    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Welsh kingdom formed in the old territory of the Cornovii after Roman rule ended, centred on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-severn">Severn</a> and the hills of mid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>.</li><li>For three centuries it bore the brunt of Mercian expansion, losing its eastern lowlands; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a>’s Dyke was built along its frontier.</li><li>Its dynasty revived under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bleddyn-ap-cynfyn">Bleddyn ap Cynfyn</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madog-ap-maredudd">Madog ap Maredudd</a>, but the kingdom was divided into two after Madog died in 1160.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The inscription on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pillar-of-eliseg">Pillar of Eliseg</a> is now almost illegible; its text is known mainly because the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-lhuyd">Edward Lhuyd</a> copied it in 1696.</p></aside>
    </section>

